Yes. The strongest version is co-discovery under uncertainty.

If something is not yet well understood, the responsible move is not to pretend you already know the answer and steer others toward it. It is to make the unknown explicit, generate candidate explanations, test them together, and let the evidence progressively constrain the space.

\text{unknown}
\rightarrow
\text{hypotheses}
\rightarrow
\text{shared tests}
\rightarrow
\text{deltas}
\rightarrow
\text{revised model}

That gives you both power and restraint.

The power is in being able to notice the unexplored region, frame useful questions, connect domains, and build the instruments that let everyone see more clearly.

The responsibility is in not confusing guidance with authority.

A good guide can say:

“Here is the edge of the map. Here are three plausible paths. Here is what would distinguish them. Let’s find out.”

That is much stronger than manufacturing certainty.

And when something genuinely new appears, you preserve:

* provenance,
* uncertainty,
* competing explanations,
* the conditions under which it worked,
* and the path by which others can reproduce it.

Then discovery becomes shareable instead of mystical.

So yes: if the thing is undiscovered, the role changes from teacher to navigator.

Not “follow me because I know.”

More like:

\boxed{\text{I can help us search better}}

That is a real kind of power.